A||N||23||kinboyatuwo||Grand am brakes and Steering Q's||||5||
Z||000000||kinboyatuwo||04-03-2001||02:54 PM||||first there is a lot of talk about the grand am brakes and i have to redo all the rotors adn probibly calipers in a month or so.   Is this a hard upgrade.  Should i go with stock?  I will do all the work myself and am just wondering?  Is it cheaper?<P><BR>Second <BR>My steering wheel has a lot of play and is progressively getting worse.  i will tear it apart soon and look bu i think it is in the tilt mechanism.  When moving the linkge in the car is not the one with play (under the dash).  I need to fix this in the near future. Hope it is not too expensive!<BR>any help would be appreciated?<P>------------------<BR>"Friends don't let friends drive four doors."||||reg||5
Z||000001||terryk||04-03-2001||06:13 PM||||I did the front GA upgrade and played with some different MC's and proportioning valves. <P>After living with it for about a year, I'm going to re-install the stock system. I have an identical 86 SE that is stock so I have something to compare to. <P>Using high quality pads and rotors, the stock brakes feel and perform better. <P>I'm not saying that none of the upgrades are good, but unless you do an "MC through to the rotors" upgrade, the the results are not worth it and in some cases worse. <P>Unless you intend to do some SERIOUS stopping (full throttle brake slams), put high quality rotors and pads on your car and you will be happy. I wish the 84-87's had the 88 brakes, but they don't, but they aren't all that bad either with good rotors and pads.||||reg||1
Z||000002||MrPBody||04-03-2001||07:48 PM||||I'll second that brake opinion.<P>Ogre has raised significant technical questions about the Grand Am upgrade.  (See the Cave and past posts.)  Also, the Grand Am upgrade set the wheels farther outboard, which makes steering heavier; heavier calipers and rotors increase unsprung weight, which is not favorable for handling on rough surfaces.<P>With new high-zoot brake pads (Carbotech Super Street) my '85 SE stops quite well.<P>I have seen a couple of recent posts about sloppy tilt-wheel steering.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||04-03-2001||07:52 PM||||Info about the steering column is also in my cave with links to Oliver's in germany where a good set of pictures is posted.<P>The Grand Am "upgrade" is much more involved than many people have been lead to believe. There are all kinds of issues. Read the "Brake Upgrade Math" thread and the big article in my cave. (The cave article is usually easier to read if you print it. Unless you have a big monitor....)<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 04-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||04-03-2001||07:52 PM||||Well, I love my Grand Am brakes and I ain't never goin' back to the wimpy stock brakes. It's a lot cheaper than a complete stock brake rebuild too.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||danny boy||04-03-2001||07:57 PM|||| It is definately cheaper, but Do it all at once or not at all. I tried just calipers/rotors first and ended up almost totaling the car. Very dangerous. <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/PFF_sig_2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||jch82||04-03-2001||08:20 PM||||I just did my front brakes (not Grand Am upgrade) earlier today....Bought pads from Advanced Auto Parts, machined the old rotors, and greased up the bearings and what not. All in all, I had max 20$ and about 2hrs labor into the entire ordeal. I plan on doing the back brakes very soon. How difficult are they to do??<P>Josh   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Yeah, my girlfriend takes collect calls from the road<BR>And it doesn't seem to matter that I'm lacking in the bulge<BR>She laughs at my dumb jokes when no one does<BR>She brings me mexican food from Sombrero's just because<BR>                 <I>-Blink 182</I><p>[This message has been edited by jch82 (edited 04-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||sjp777||04-03-2001||08:32 PM||||Your steering wheel problem is the 4 bolts that hold the tilt mechanism to the rest of the column. They come loose usually as a result of pounding on the steering wheel in traffic frustration. They are a total wh*re to get to. You have to pull the wheel, which requires a tool, pull the lock plate, which requires a different tool, then the turn signal assembly and arm and the ignition lock cylinder. Then, if you look deep into the column, you just might see these 4 bolts with weird reverse torx heads that require a torx socket to tighten. Don't just tighten them, pull them out and put red loctite on before reinstalling. Trust me, if you have to do this once, you will never want to do it again. I've had to do it twice and have never been able to get at the 4th bolt. If you can get 3, consider yourself lucky. Getting the turn signal assembly back together will probably give you an ulcer. Have a 12 pack handy before starting<p>[This message has been edited by sjp777 (edited 04-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||kinboyatuwo||04-03-2001||09:43 PM||||Thanks for all the help<P>i think stock rotors and good pads are going on<P>might rebuild the calipers<P>as for the steering problem i will look at fixing that next week and leave a full day open for all the work<P>thanks to all<P>any other hints is appreciated<P>and i will read the info in the cave, used it before and is alway s the best source for info!!!<P>------------------<BR>"Friends don't let friends drive four doors."||||reg||7
Z||000009||Will||04-04-2001||11:00 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by sjp777:<BR><B>Your steering wheel problem is the 4 bolts that hold the tilt mechanism to the rest of the column. They come loose usually as a result of pounding on the steering wheel in traffic frustration. They are a total wh*re to get to. You have to pull the wheel, which requires a tool, pull the lock plate, which requires a different tool, then the turn signal assembly and arm and the ignition lock cylinder. Then, if you look deep into the column, you just might see these 4 bolts with weird reverse torx heads that require a torx socket to tighten. Don't just tighten them, pull them out and put red loctite on before reinstalling. Trust me, if you have to do this once, you will never want to do it again. I've had to do it twice and have never been able to get at the 4th bolt. If you can get 3, consider yourself lucky. Getting the turn signal assembly back together will probably give you an ulcer. Have a 12 pack handy before starting<P>[This message has been edited by sjp777 (edited 04-03-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>All four bolts are easy to get to if you tear the column completely down (involves removing pivot pins--theogre recently posted a link to a tool to do that for less than $20).<BR>O really don't see the point of locktiting them if they take close to 100K miles to work lose in the first place. Byt the time they work lose again you'll have to be into your column for something else anyway.||||reg||1
Z||000010||mrfixit58||04-04-2001||03:46 PM||||Unless you have the tools, there MAY be a cheaper and easier method. My column was the same way and I have all the tools to disassemble to column but chose a different approach. I checked the local U-Pull-It yard and found several Fieros with good steering columns. I pulled the entire steering column (tok about 5 minutes) and paid them $10 plus a $2 core charge and just r&r-ed the old column. Took less time (since I was going to the J-yard anyway) and costs less than buying the tools.<P>Just a thought,<BR>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||3
Z||000011||JR||04-04-2001||08:38 PM||||Have GrandAm brakes on the rear of my 85GT, best thing I ever did, total cost? 138 CDN$ for vented rotors and calipers with pads.<P>Fronts seem to last, on third set of pads, and pistons still retract easily to accomodate new pads--just bleed the breaks regularly.<P>Don't notice any real improvement in brake performance with GrandAm's on the rear,  as I'm not hard on this car, but occassionally have tried a panic stop and the pedal feels somwhat firmer.  For city driving you can't beat the price.  For hard driving I 'll leave to my 88GT.<P>------------------<BR>85GT (daily driver 120,000 miles)<BR>88GT (highly modified sunday driver)||||reg||1
Z||000012||kinboyatuwo||04-04-2001||08:43 PM||||hey JR <P>where did you get the stuff for that price<BR>is that per side or what<BR>rotors alone would me that much almost for stock<P>email info<P>also interested in getting together<BR>a few others are around here and are wiling to com<P>------------------<BR>"Friends don't let friends drive four doors."<P>87 Duke, 235,000km||||reg||1
Z||000013||JR||04-04-2001||08:47 PM||||Oh, btw Ogre.  I mean no disrespect.  I admire all the research you have done on the fiero brake system, and I concur with most of it.  But, grand am's on the rear only, I feel are safe but probably illegal due to the absense of a parking brake.  I have tried them hard occasionally and have had no premature lockup or stability problems.  What gives?  Please explain the expected problems with this upgrade in drivers terms.<P>------------------<BR>85GT (daily driver 120,000 miles)<BR>88GT (highly modified sunday driver)||||reg||1
Z||000014||danny boy||04-04-2001||08:54 PM||||I must have done something wrong.When I put the G/A on rear only I almost wrecked the car trying them out and I had all kinds of mushy pedal. They were bled more than enough times.<BR> Oh well, The 88 brakes are gonna be much better.||||reg||1
Z||000015||theogre||04-05-2001||08:29 PM||||JR, <P>The rear upgrade can never be made street legal because of the hand brake issue. This is a USDOT rule. I'm told it is the same for Canada but I've not taken the time to verify it.<P>A rear only upgrade is very deceptive. Allot of it has to do with the proportioning valve. If you reprop the OE brakes then you would feel similar results.<P>In most cases Vented Rear rotors are not needed. The rear axle doesn't generate the heat in the brakes that the front does. It's nice to have but it's probably not doing very much for the majority of people using them. (Only the racers will likely generate enough heat for it to matter.)<P>To make maters worse, If you still have stock front brakes, then the rears are now capable generating way more clamp than the fronts. If the Prop valve ever screws up then you could easily find your self pointing wrong way down the road. There's no way I'd ever trust a car with a rear only GA conversion. (Prop valves do go bad...)<P>You said you tried them hard a couple times... did you do that on wet road? Snow? Dirt? Brakes that work on dry road can be really nasty when it get's slick out. Especially when you stomp them hard. You could still find they lock very unexpectedly depending on road conditions.<P>If you compare Fiero rears to other rear disk cars the price of parts really isn't that bad. it's often less than many. (Except maybe for the 88)<P>||||reg||1
Z||000016||filthyscarecrow||04-06-2001||02:43 AM||||if you want brakes that don't fade, get some cross drilled rotors or something. i'm not so sure i trust the OE brakes on a race track after what happened at elkhart last year. i'm lucky i know how to heel-toe or i wouldn't have made the corner...<BR>you could also buy some race grade neoprene or silicone brake ducting and run ducts from the front of the car to the front brakes to cool them off down the straights. i'm considering that as my first attempt at fixing the problem before i go back to elkhart this summer. hopefully that'll do it. i've thought of other ways too. like using a leafblower rigged into the electrical system to blow cold air on the brakes all the time (at least when i'm going really fast...) yeah, i'm crazy. what of it? i have to go wash my hands a lot now. <P>------------------<BR>allow me to show you my exhaust pipes...||||reg||1
Z||000017||Thecrewchief1||04-06-2001||02:54 AM||||I did my brakes (90 Beretta) last month and at first I only did the rears. Talk about to much rear brake! I have to make a u-turn to get my shop and all I had to do was hit the brakes and turn left and I was facing the other way.I next did the fronts and put a 93 Blazer M/C on, new wheel bearings, seals and 4 new rubber lines, much better. All pads are Performance Friction Carbon Metallic and the fluid is Valvolne synthetic (I use this stuff in my limited late model and it works great). Everything works great, but the thing about the smaller bore M/C makes sense. I think the front and rear bores should be the same size or close. Total cost was about $350 for everything, thats not to bad for what I got. Thanks Bill||||reg||1
Z||000018||theogre||04-06-2001||01:32 PM||||A couple quick coments. (I'm at work...)<P>FSCrow,<P>The OE brakes aren't built for race use. I'm not suprised you had trouble with them. I'm still working on how to use alternate parts to get vented front brakes without the fit problems GE rotors have.<P>Bill,<P>"Everything works great, but the thing about the smaller bore M/C makes sense. I think the front and rear bores should be the same size or close. "<P>Did you mean the front and rear scetions of the MC?<P>I think you miss undertood how a step bore MC works. The portion of the MC that does the actual brake work is the same diameter for both circuits. The large bore only provides volume to take up slack in the calipers. ||||reg||1
Z||000019||Cplensdorf||04-06-2001||10:50 PM||||For what it is worth:<P>I installed Grand Am brakes on the rear of my 86 SE around 25,000 miles ago.  I have had no problems at all.  I DO drive the vehicle relatively hard in all types of Michigan weather (snow, rain, etc.)  I NEVER experienced the problems that have been stated in this post.  I felt a definite improvement on the ability to stop the vehicle and the entire rebuild of the rear brakes cost under $100 US.  As far as DOT regulations, is it better to have a 'locked-up' parking brake and misadjusted rear brakes, as almost all Fieros on the road have(I replaced mine three times in the seven years I have owned the car!...and set it EVERY time I stopped the car) or not have a parking brake at all with brakes that stop the vehicle quicker?  Don't know.  I now own an 88GT and do notice a big difference in the performance of the brakes, though.  From my experience, the proportioning issue is more theory (and the rear lockups, poor driving skills) than reality and I would recommend this upgrade to anyone looking for an affordable replacement to the stock configuration.||||reg||1
Z||000020||filthyscarecrow||04-07-2001||12:05 AM||||you're right ogre, i'm not surprised that the brakes faded at the track. i WAS surprised at the severity of the problem though. one would think that brake fade would be noticable, but not damn near catastrophic. when i pushed the pedal for the next corner, there was NOTHING there. nadda, zip... so i had to downshift. the thing that surprised me most is that they felt fine even in the previous corner, and had already done 2 or 3 laps. it was almost instantaneous. i wasn't like they went away, it's like they vanished. part of my problem was the brake fluid, but it was still surprising just how bad the problem was. i know they aren't made for racing, but i wasn't "racing," only driving hard. <P>incedentally, ogre, what are your thoughts on drilling the rotors yourself? i do have access to a CNC machine and i've done a little work on Pro Engineer, so i could draw up a Pro-E model and just have the CNC machine (or a machine shop) do it. do you think that would weaken the rotors too much? to me it seems a little risky of a thing to do, but i know there are umpteen million people selling rotors elsewhere that began life as blanks and they drilled/slotted them themselves. would it be okay if the rotor being drilled were a high quality blank like Brembo or something? <P>------------------<BR>allow me to show you my exhaust pipes...||||reg||1
Z||000021||Songman||04-07-2001||02:31 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by theogre:<BR><B>If you compare Fiero rears to other rear disk cars the price of parts really isn't that bad. it's often less than many. (Except maybe for the 88)</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I put new pads and rotors on the back of my 88. Through some nice advice from this board I found that I could use 96 Cavalier front rotors on the rear of my Fiero. The Cavalier rotors costing $13 each work just as well as the Fiero rotors at $70 each. Gotta love this forum!<P>What I did find is that the brake system on 88 Fieros are a b*tch to work on. Who came up with that funky screw-in system on the calipers. Even with the right tool, the slot is not deep enough to catch to turn the caliper in. Spent most of my day just on that.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000022||theogre||04-07-2001||01:04 PM||||FSCrow,<P>Drilling/slotting aren't cooling methods. Infact Wilwood says they can do just the oposite. Check their FAQ. the link to them is in the upgrade article near the bottom.<P>In your case, it sounds allot like the brake fluid flash boiled. I'd flush it out and try Valvoline synthetic fluid. Compress all the calipers to work out as much old fluid as you can. Better yet take the lines off and flip the front ones over so they can drain. You can do that to the rear but it's a big pain... Makes for allot of bleeding but you want as much old fluid out as you can get. Expect to work at least a quart of fluid thru the system.<P>I'm still looking for a better vented rotor to base the front upgrade on. It's going to be awhile. Once the right rotor is found we should be able to get them a better fit on the hub.||||reg||1
Z||000023||lowCG||04-08-2001||01:38 AM||||I have a new set of rotors from a '90s Subaru Legacy,that are 10.5" diameter,.950" thick,with a 58mm bore.My plan was to try and install these on the front first with some BMW/Volvo 4 piston calipers,although I'm sure there would be a serious penalty by having added so much unsprung weight to the car.<BR>I don't race the car,so it hasn't really been a problem with my lousy stock brakes.<BR>||||reg||1
